title: 'No connect timeout: a socket that never accepts still hangs'
updated: 2026-08-27T22:21:38Z
---

Noticed while implementing the read timeout (#59), deliberately left out of it.

#59 added read_timeout, which is SO_RCVTIMEO on the connected socket. It bounds
reading and nothing else. _build__socket still passes no Timeout to any of the
three constructors:

  IO::Socket::UNIX->new(Peer => $path, Type => SOCK_STREAM)
  IO::Socket::INET->new(PeerAddr => ..., PeerPort => ..., Proto => 'tcp')
  IO::Socket::SSL->new(PeerAddr => ..., PeerPort => ..., Proto => 'tcp', %ssl)

So a daemon that accepts the connection and then says nothing is now caught,
while a tcp:// host that drops SYNs is not -- that blocks in connect(2) for the
kernel's own timeout, which on Linux is over two minutes. Writing the request is
unbounded too (SO_SNDTIMEO), though that only matters for a large /build context
against a daemon that has stopped reading.

Cheap to add (Timeout => N on all three constructors) but it is a separate
decision: it needs its own attribute, or an explicit statement that read_timeout
covers connecting as well, which today it does not and its POD says so.

Also unmeasured here: IO::Socket::SSL's Timeout covers the TCP connect but not
necessarily the whole handshake.

Related: #59.
